-- title:
--   Eq. (7) — rotational covariance of $\Lambda_{1/2}$ and $\Lambda_1$
-- statement:
--   The isotropic channels of eq. (6) commute with rotations of the spin:
--
--   $$
--   \Lambda_s\big(U_s(\mathbf n)\,\rho\,U_s^\dagger(\mathbf n)\big)=U_s(\mathbf n)\,\Lambda_s(\rho)\,U_s^\dagger(\mathbf n).
--   $$
--
--   Precisely:
--
--   1. for $s=1/2$, $\Lambda_{1/2}(UXU^\dagger)=U\Lambda_{1/2}(X)U^\dagger$ for every unitary $2\times2$ matrix $U$ and every $2\times2$ matrix $X$ (the paper notes on p.2 that at $s=1/2$, (7) is invariance under all unitaries);
--   2. for $s=1$, $\Lambda_1(UXU^\dagger)=U\Lambda_1(X)U^\dagger$ for every $U=\exp(i\,\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf S)=\exp\big(i\sum_k n_kS_k\big)$, $\mathbf n\in\mathbb R^3$, with $S_k$ the matrices (13), and every $3\times3$ matrix $X$.
--
--   Covariance is what lets the proof of Theorem 1 move an input or the matrix $Y$ to a normal form.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `NormedSpace.exp` is the matrix exponential; its value does not depend on the matrix norm used to elaborate it.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: s = 1/2: invariance under U_{1/2}(n) → invariance under every unitary U of ℂ²; reason: the page itself reads (7) at s = 1/2 as invariance under all unitaries (p.2); specializes to U_{1/2}(n)
--   - generalization: s = 1: U_1(n), n ∈ R³, |n| ≤ 1, the spin-1 representation of SU(2) → U = exp(i Σ_k n_k S_k) for every n ∈ ℝ³; reason: the spin-1 representation generated by (13) is {exp(i n·S)}; every n is allowed, which contains the page's |n| ≤ 1
--   - generalization: ρ a density matrix → every square matrix X; reason: both sides are linear in X; specializes to density matrices
-- source:
--   Alicki, Isotropic quantum spin channels and additivity questions, arXiv:quant-ph/0402080v1, eq. (7), p.1; §2, Case s = 1/2, p.2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_IsotropicSpin_spinHalf
import Definitions.Def_IsotropicSpin_spinOne
import Definitions.Def_IsotropicSpin_isotropicChannel

open scoped Matrix

namespace IsotropicSpin

/-- **Rotational invariance (7)**, Alicki, arXiv:quant-ph/0402080v1, eq. (7), p. 1, for `s = 1/2`
and `s = 1`: `Λ_s(U_s(n) ρ U_s†(n)) = U_s(n) Λ_s(ρ) U_s†(n)`.

1. For `s = 1/2` the invariance holds for every unitary `U` of `ℂ²` (the page's own reading of
   (7) at `s = 1/2`, §2, p. 2: "the invariance with respect to all unitary transformation").
2. For `s = 1` it holds for every `U = exp(i n·S) = exp(i Σ_k n_k S_k)`, `n ∈ ℝ³`, the spin-1
   representation of `SU(2)` generated by the matrices (13).

**Formalization Note** Both identities are stated for every square matrix `X` (the maps are
linear). `NormedSpace.exp` is the matrix exponential (it does not depend on a choice of norm). -/
theorem eq_7 :
    (∀ U ∈ Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin 2) ℂ, ∀ X : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ,
      isotropicChannel (1 / 2) spinHalf (U * X * Uᴴ) =
        U * isotropicChannel (1 / 2) spinHalf X * Uᴴ) ∧
    (∀ (n : Fin 3 → ℝ) (X : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) ℂ),
      isotropicChannel 1 spinOne
          (NormedSpace.exp (Complex.I • ∑ k, (n k : ℂ) • spinOne k) * X *
            (NormedSpace.exp (Complex.I • ∑ k, (n k : ℂ) • spinOne k))ᴴ) =
        NormedSpace.exp (Complex.I • ∑ k, (n k : ℂ) • spinOne k) * isotropicChannel 1 spinOne X *
          (NormedSpace.exp (Complex.I • ∑ k, (n k : ℂ) • spinOne k))ᴴ) := by sorry

end IsotropicSpin
